WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:04.840
It looks like the House of Mouse has delivered
a live-action fable worthy of a king.

00:00:05.020 --> 00:00:10.180
First reactions are pouring in for Disney's
live-action remake of the classic 1994 animated

00:00:10.190 --> 00:00:14.260
film The Lion King, and they're full of nothing
but praise for the movie's stunning visuals

00:00:14.260 --> 00:00:16.160
and brilliant voice performances.

00:00:16.740 --> 00:00:18.280
"Oh, goody."

00:00:19.060 --> 00:00:23.260
That The Lion King is being heralded as a
landmark in photorealistic computer animation

00:00:23.260 --> 00:00:24.700
shouldn't come as a surprise.

00:00:24.780 --> 00:00:28.119
The movie is the handiwork of director Jon
Favreau, who pulled off something

00:00:28.120 --> 00:00:31.760
similar with his 2016 live-action remake of
The Jungle Book.

00:00:32.080 --> 00:00:36.980
At the time, that movie was also widely praised
for its deeply detailed and meticulously rendered

00:00:36.980 --> 00:00:37.980
CGI characters.

00:00:38.160 --> 00:00:42.860
For The Lion King, Favreau worked with a screenplay
by Jeff Nathanson, who previously worked on

00:00:42.860 --> 00:00:47.600
Catch Me If You Can, The Terminal and Pirates
of the Caribbean: Dead Men Tell No Tales.

00:00:47.720 --> 00:00:52.020
He also had the pleasure of presiding over
a cast that included some of Hollywood's hottest

00:00:52.020 --> 00:00:52.520
stars.

00:00:52.540 --> 00:00:56.320
Folks who caught preview screenings for The
Lion King quickly flocked to Twitter to heap

00:00:56.320 --> 00:00:57.420
praise on the flick.

00:00:57.570 --> 00:01:01.540
Actress and cosplayer extraordinaire Mica
Burton gushed over the movie's faithfulness

00:01:01.540 --> 00:01:05.280
to the original, while expressing appreciation
for its more modern touches.

00:01:05.280 --> 00:01:05.780
She wrote:

00:01:05.900 --> 00:01:08.500
"The Lion King… was more than anything I
ever could've wanted.

00:01:08.500 --> 00:01:12.640
It was BEAUTIFULLY animated, paid the highest
of honors to the original, and was packed

00:01:12.649 --> 00:01:13.740
with modern humor."

00:01:13.740 --> 00:01:18.560
The Wrap senior film reporter Beatrice Verhoeven
was in full agreement, bursting with praise

00:01:18.560 --> 00:01:22.659
for pretty much every aspect of the film,
in particular the performance of a certain

00:01:22.660 --> 00:01:23.960
pop music powerhouse.

00:01:24.220 --> 00:01:24.780
She tweeted:

00:01:24.960 --> 00:01:27.100
"Oh man #TheLionKing delivers.

00:01:27.100 --> 00:01:30.929
It's a visual masterpiece that will leave
you smiling and crying the whole time.

00:01:30.929 --> 00:01:34.499
It's a true testament to the lasting effect
Disney movies have on all generations.

00:01:34.620 --> 00:01:36.780
Timon and Pumbaa steal the show.

00:01:36.780 --> 00:01:37.580
And BEYONCE!!!"

00:01:37.800 --> 00:01:42.120
Screen Picks editor and critic Kit Bowen acknowledged
that the original film set a high bar to live

00:01:42.120 --> 00:01:45.200
up to but admitted the remake won her over
nonetheless.

00:01:45.360 --> 00:01:45.860
She wrote:

00:01:46.020 --> 00:01:48.240
"Did we need a live-action version of #TheLionKing?

00:01:48.460 --> 00:01:48.960
Maybe not.

00:01:49.140 --> 00:01:53.740
The OG Lion King still holds my heart forever,
but this version is beautifully crafted by

00:01:53.749 --> 00:01:58.409
Jon Favreau, aptly voiced by great talent
and [gave] me all the feels [of] being back

00:01:58.409 --> 00:01:59.400
on Pride Rock."

00:01:59.740 --> 00:02:03.590
Collider's Steve Weintraub wrote that the
house came down at his screening, and predicted

00:02:03.590 --> 00:02:05.780
a colossal box office win for Disney.

00:02:05.880 --> 00:02:06.400
He tweeted:

00:02:06.600 --> 00:02:10.440
"Been lucky to have attended a number of world
premieres but not sure I've ever heard as

00:02:10.440 --> 00:02:13.480
much clapping during a movie as I did tonight
at #TheLionKing.

00:02:13.600 --> 00:02:15.220
Was like being at a rock concert.

00:02:15.380 --> 00:02:17.320
Movie is going to make so much money."

00:02:17.620 --> 00:02:22.280
One of the few criticisms of the movie came
from ScreenRant and Forbes critic Alisha Grauso

00:02:22.290 --> 00:02:23.290
who first tweeted:

00:02:23.290 --> 00:02:24.600
"The Lion King is a dazzler.

00:02:24.760 --> 00:02:28.980
Gorgeous score, spot-on voice acting, but
mostly, yeah, those visuals."

00:02:28.980 --> 00:02:30.100
She then followed up with:

00:02:30.230 --> 00:02:34.700
"That being said, #TheLionKing does illustrate
the limitations of projecting human emotions

00:02:34.700 --> 00:02:38.160
onto photorealistic animals that can't convey
human expression."

00:02:38.290 --> 00:02:43.180
Also less than enthralled with the movie was
Mashable editor Angie J. Han, who questioned

00:02:43.190 --> 00:02:47.489
the value of such an uber-faithful remake
while conceding that a lot of skill went into

00:02:47.489 --> 00:02:47.980
making it.

00:02:48.240 --> 00:02:48.740
She wrote:

00:02:48.920 --> 00:02:53.240
"#TheLionKing is exactly as advertised: a
beat-for-beat remake of the original.

00:02:53.440 --> 00:02:58.120
Impressive animation and some ace casting
choices [...] plus that music is still [great]

00:02:58.120 --> 00:03:01.100
— but it's more nostalgic rehash than fresh
reimagining."

00:03:02.540 --> 00:03:04.060
"Talk about your fixer-upper."

00:03:04.680 --> 00:03:08.820
Overall, though, those fortunate enough to
catch the flick early were absolutely blown

00:03:08.820 --> 00:03:09.320
away.

00:03:09.420 --> 00:03:13.940
All in all, it sure seems like The Lion King
is destined for critical and box office glory

00:03:13.940 --> 00:03:17.300
and is likely to be counted as yet another
huge win for Disney.

